{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Visiting the iniquity of fathers upon children</b> Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said that the meaning of פקידה (visiting) is like the meaning of זכירה (remembering), as in \"And HaShem visited Sarah\" (Genesis 21:1), which is like \"And HaShem remembered her\" (I Samuel 1:19). And the explanation is that HaShem may prolong  the life of the wicked man — perhaps he will repent and beget a righteous son — but if the son walks in his father's ways, then the third generation and the fourth generation too will be destroyed, their memory obliterated. For HaShem remembers what the fathers did, and no longer prolongs. Thus said all the commentators. If so, He does not visit the iniquity of fathers upon children and upon the third generation, but only upon the fourth, and it would have been fitting for Him to say: \"visiting the iniquity of fathers and children and the third generation upon the fourth.\" Or perhaps they will say that He remembers the iniquity upon the children, saying: \"You and your father have sinned,\" and so He does with the third generation and with the fourth, and then He takes vengeance upon them and does not visit upon them further, for He cuts them off in the iniquity of them all. But their explanation is not correct, for Scripture would have mentioned remembrance equally upon all of them, and would not place the vengeance that falls upon the fourth at the end. Moreover, the expression פקידה with the word עַל does not denote remembering but rather vengeance, as in \"and in the day of My visitation I will visit their sin upon them\" (Exodus 32:34), and likewise \"HaShem will visit with His hard and great and strong sword upon Leviathan the fleeing serpent...\" (Isaiah 27:1), and likewise \"HaShem will visit upon the host of the height in the height\" (Isaiah 24:21) — all of these are vengeance and punishment.\n\nAnd the correct view in my eyes is that He visits the iniquity that the father committed upon his children and cuts them off in their fathers' iniquity, in the sense of what is said: \"Prepare slaughter for his children because of the iniquity of their fathers\" (Isaiah 14:21). And so He visits it upon the third generation, when their iniquity is not yet complete in two generations, in the sense of \"for the iniquity of the Amorite is not yet complete\" (Genesis 15:16). And sometimes He visits the iniquity of all upon the fourth generation, when their measure is full, and cuts them off; but in the fifth generation the son is not punished for the iniquity of the original father. And in the repetition of the Torah (Deuteronomy 5:9) He adds \"upon the third generation and upon the fourth generation, to those who hate Me,\" as though to say: to them.\n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said that grandchildren are called children (בנים); therefore he took a shortcut, for from the word שלשים (third generation) and רבעים (fourth generation) one understands this. But this is not so; rather, שלשים is the third generation in the iniquity, and רבעים likewise, for they are four sins. And the verse that says in the Thirteen Attributes (Exodus 34:7), \"visiting the iniquity of fathers upon children and upon children's children, upon the third generation and upon the fourth,\" should be explained as referring to grandchildren who are the third and the fourth. Therefore Moses returned in the passage of the spies and said, \"upon children, upon the third generation and upon the fourth\" (Numbers 14:18), and did not mention children's children — and it is all one.\n\nAnd Scripture says <b>to those who hate Me</b>, when the children are haters of HaShem, for if he begets a righteous son he will not bear the iniquity of the father, as Ezekiel explained (Ezekiel 18:20). And from the words of our Sages (Mekhilta here) it appears as the explanation I have given, for they derived from here that the measure of good is multiplied beyond the measure of punishment — the measure of punishment is for four generations, etc. — and if the explanation I gave first were so, the measure of good would be even more multiplied if He prolongs even to the tenth generation. And it is possible that this severe measure applies to idolatry alone, for concerning it He warns; but in the case of other commandments, \"every man shall die for his own iniquity\" (Jeremiah 31:29).\n\nAnd the hidden secret in \"visiting the iniquity of fathers upon children\" at the beginning of the book of Ecclesiastes (1:4) you will find, and I have already written it (Genesis 38:8)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The text appears corrupt at 'כטעם או' — literally 'like the meaning of: or.' Rendered hyper-literally as 'as though to say: to them,' reading 'או' as an attempted gloss on the added phrase in Deuteronomy."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:11:37.599824482Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}